Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Bombard**, a four-legged siege mortar walker that
strides on legs, swivels a turret to aim, and lobs a long mortar barrel high — as a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You paint discrete
opaque cells to build it and you **author its walk and weapon animations** as
F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Bombard is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the walker into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking,
firing artillery walker needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and renders
**nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled
scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), set their pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints on them
with `define-joint`, and author the required animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with
`--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is a
**separate field** sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates. The volume starts
**empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim fill-box --part hull --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole walker composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together and that the legs reach the ground. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on
the finished walker; the scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the walker up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a low, boxy hull raised on legs that reach the
ground at the corners, a turret sitting on top, and a long mortar barrel projecting
forward from the turret. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the
`voxel-anim` animation subcommands: `walk` (a four-legged gait whose feet plant flat
and still, then lift and swing — with weight, not a weightless slide) and
`bombard_fire` (the barrel kicking up in a recoil-lob and settling while the legs
hold planted). The animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by those names;
the parts and joints that realize them are yours. When you are satisfied, stop: the
recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
